{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GSLLXgeMpfMq52h6uBF64e","type":"tool_result","content":"2:## *\"Read the phase before you read the logs\"*\n13:## Attention Budget\n47:## 🧭 Soundings\n53:1. A Pod's `status` reports a `Reason` string of `ContainerCreating`. Does that string belong to the Pod's **phase** taxonomy or the **container state** taxonomy?\n55:2. A Pod has been sitting in `Pending` for ten minutes. Is some component of the cluster periodically retrying it with relaxed constraints, hoping to eventually place it?\n57:3. A Pod spec names the image `myapp` with no tag at all. What `imagePullPolicy` does Kubernetes apply by default, and when does the kubelet consult the registry?\n59:4. A Pod has two containers. Container A requests 100m CPU and 128Mi memory, with limits of 200m and 256Mi. Container B requests 50m CPU and 64Mi memory, with no limits at all. What QoS class does this Pod receive?\n61:5. A node reports `Ready=False`. What is that condition actually telling you, and which component wrote it?\n63:6. Your control plane runs Kubernetes 1.37. What is the oldest kubelet minor version that is supported against it?\n65:7. A Pod runs three containers: `app`, `cache`, and `log-shipper`. How do you ask for the logs of `cache` specifically?\n67:8. Someone creates an Ingress object on a cluster where no Ingress controller has ever been installed. Does the object get created? Does anything route traffic?\n69:<details>\n72:**1.** Container state. `Reason` is a field on the container state, not on the Pod phase. The five Pod phases are `Pending`, `Running`, `Succeeded`, `Failed`, `Unknown`, and none of them is called `ContainerCreating`. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 §5 — Pod phases and container states]*\n74:**2.** No. Nothing is retrying it with looser constraints. `Pending` is a stable report that no feasible node was found. The scheduler will place the Pod the moment a node becomes feasible, but it will not relax the Pod's own requirements to make that happen. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 7 §2 — feasible nodes]*\n76:**3.** With no tag, Kubernetes assumes `:latest`, and the default `imagePullPolicy` for `:latest` is `Always`. The kubelet queries the registry every time it launches a container. [source: k8s-docs-images-2026-08-23] *[cross-bearing: see Ch 2 §6 — imagePullPolicy]*\n78:**4.** `Burstable`. It is not `Guaranteed`, because container B has no limits and container A's limits do not equal its requests. It is not `BestEffort`, because at least one container has requests. [source: k8s-docs-pod-qos-2026-08-24] *[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 §8 — QoS classes]*\n80:**5.** `Ready=False` means the node is not healthy and is not accepting Pods. The kubelet reports node status; the node controller sets `Ready` to `Unknown` if it stops hearing from the node entirely. [source: k8s-docs-node-status-2026-08-24] *[cross-bearing: see Ch 8 §4 — node conditions]*\n82:**6.** 1.34. The kubelet may be up to three minor versions older than the API server, and must never be newer. [source: k8s-version-skew-policy-2026-08-31] *[cross-bearing: see Ch 8 §6 — the version-skew window]*\n84:**7.** `kubectl logs <pod> -c cache`. Without `-c`, the command has to guess, and on a multi-container Pod it will refuse or pick the first container rather than the one you meant. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 §2 — multi-container Pods]*\n86:**8.** The object is created. The API server accepts it and stores it. Nothing routes traffic, because no controller is watching for Ingress objects and acting on them. The object is a record of intent with nobody to honor it. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 10 §3 — the object exists; nothing happens without the component]*\n96:</details>\n100:## Why This Chapter Matters\n128:## What You'll Learn\n143:## ⚪ §1 — Whose Problem Is This, and What to Read First\n198:## 🔵 §2 — Pods That Never Start\n331:## ⚪ §3 — Looking Inside\n451:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings: Pods That Never Start, and the Commands That Tell You Why\n455:**1.** A Pod's containers show `Reason: CreateContainerConfigError`. Which of the following is definitely true?\n462:**2.** You run `kubectl get pods` and see a Pod with `STATUS: ImagePullBackOff` and `RESTARTS: 0`. What does the restart count of zero tell you?\n469:**3.** A Pod has been in `Pending` for twenty minutes. Which single command output most directly tells you why?\n476:**4.** Your `kubectl apply` of a Deployment succeeded, but no Pods exist and the Deployment shows zero available replicas. Where is the explanation most likely to be?\n483:**5.** A Pod spec sets `imagePullPolicy: Never` and names an image that exists in your registry but not on the node. What happens?\n490:**6.** *[retrieval: ch2]* You want a Pod to run one exact, unchangeable build of an image, with no possibility that a moved pointer silently gives you different content. Do you reference the image by tag or by digest, and why?\n499:<details>\n569:</details>\n573:## 🔵 §4 — Pods That Start and Then Don't Stay\n723:## 🟡 §5 — When the Node Is the Problem\n829:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings: Started, Then Gone — and the Node Beneath\n833:**1.** A container's last state shows `Terminated` with `Reason: OOMKilled`, and the Pod is `Running` with a restart count of 4. What happened, and where is the Pod now?\n840:**2.** A Pod shows `Running`, `0/1` in the `READY` column, zero restarts, and receives no traffic. What is the most likely cause?\n847:**3.** A node has shown `Ready=Unknown` for the last two minutes. Its Pods are still listed as `Running`. Why has nothing been rescheduled yet?\n854:**4.** You run `kubectl get pods` on a node you suspect is broken and see nothing unexpected. You then run `crictl ps` on that node and find a container running that `kubectl` did not report. What does this tell you?\n861:**5.** *[retrieval: ch5]* A Pod has one container that requests 500m CPU and 512Mi memory, with limits of 500m CPU and 512Mi memory. What QoS class is it, and what does that mean for eviction order?\n870:<details>\n929:</details>\n933:## 🟡 §6 — Versions That Don't Agree\n979:## 🔵 §7 — Numbers Nobody Collects by Default\n1078:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings: Skew, Metrics, and What Isn't There\n1082:**1.** `kubectl top nodes` returns an error on a cluster where every node is `Ready` and every workload is healthy. What is the most likely explanation?\n1089:**2.** *[retrieval: ch8]* Your control plane is at 1.37. You are asked whether a node running kubelet 1.33 is supported. What is the answer, and what is the rule?\n1096:**3.** *[retrieval: ch10]* Which of the following best describes what an HPA does on a cluster with no metrics-server installed?\n1103:**4.** A container was evicted from a node four hours ago. You want to read what it logged just before it went. What should you expect?\n1110:**5.** A manifest applies cleanly against your development cluster and is rejected against production with an error about an unrecognized field. Both clusters run the same workload. What should you check before editing the manifest?\n1119:<details>\n1184:</details>\n1188:## ☀️ §8 — Read the Phase First\n1254:## Exam Alert! 🚨\n1258:1. **The never-started / started-then-died split, and which signature belongs to which.** This is the chapter in one line, and it is the discrimination the exam reaches for most. `Pending`, the image-pull family, and `CreateContainerConfigError` are never-started. `CrashLoopBackOff`, `OOMKilled`, and `Evicted` are started-then-died. The restart count tells you which side you are on.\n1260:2. **`CrashLoopBackOff` means the container ran.** The image pulled, the config resolved, the process started, and exited. It is a restart-throttling state, not a start failure.\n1262:3. **`OOMKilled` versus `Evicted`.** Different killer (kernel cgroup enforcement versus the kubelet), different scope (one container versus the whole Pod), different trigger (this Pod's own limit versus the node's pressure), different outcome (restarted in place versus rescheduled elsewhere).\n1264:4. **`kubectl top` requires metrics-server, and no cluster ships one by default.** The error is about the cluster's build, not about the workload.\n1283:## Practice Questions\n1287:**1.** A Pod has been in `Pending` for fifteen minutes. `kubectl describe pod` shows a scheduler event reading, in part, `0/6 nodes are available: 6 node(s) had untolerated taint {workload: gpu}`. What is the correct interpretation?\n1294:**2.** Which single column of `kubectl get pods` output most quickly separates a never-started failure from a started-then-died failure?\n1301:**3.** A Pod's container shows `Waiting` with `Reason: ImagePullBackOff`. Which of the following is *not* a plausible cause?\n1308:**4.** A Deployment's Pods all show `CreateContainerConfigError`. The image is correct and pullable, and the nodes have ample capacity. What should you look for?\n1315:**5.** A Pod runs a container whose memory limit is 256Mi. Under load, the process allocates 300Mi. What happens, and what is the resulting signature?\n1322:**6.** A node reports `MemoryPressure=True`. Three Pods on it are terminated. Which is terminated first?\n1329:**7.** *[interleaved: D1.1 workloads]* A Deployment shows `ProgressDeadlineExceeded` and has zero available replicas. `kubectl describe deployment` gives you the condition but no cause. What is the correct next step?\n1336:**8.** *[interleaved: D2.2 security]* A Pod will not start. Its container reports `CreateContainerConfigError`, and the Pod spec mounts a Secret named `db-credentials` as a volume. `kubectl get secret db-credentials` returns `NotFound`. What is the diagnosis, and at what stage did it fail?\n1343:**9.** A Pod shows `Running` with `1/1` in the `READY` column, and its restart count is 7. What can you conclude?\n1350:**10.** You want to read the logs of the container instance that died, not the one currently starting. Which command?\n1357:**11.** A node has shown `Ready=Unknown` for the last ten minutes. What has the node controller done, and what has happened to the Pods?\n1364:**12.** On a node you suspect of a kubelet fault, `kubectl get pods -o wide` shows two Pods on that node. `crictl ps` on the node shows four running containers belonging to Kubernetes workloads. What does the discrepancy indicate?\n1371:**13.** *[interleaved: D1.3 scheduling]* A cluster-wide feature works on every node except one. That node's kubelet is four minor versions behind the API server. Which statement is correct?\n1378:**14.** `kubectl top pod` fails on a cluster. Every workload is healthy. Which of the following would resolve it?\n1385:**15.** An engineer says: \"The Pod failed overnight and `kubectl describe` shows `Events: <none>`, so nothing went wrong with the platform — it must be an application bug.\" What is the flaw in that reasoning?\n1394:<details>\n1485:</details>\n1489:## Chapter Summary\n1519:## The Voyage Ahead","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"58ff69ea-af14-4b88-8694-2fdf821db391","uuid":"42c94d2c-34a8-461d-8d39-9e88edaa5ad9","timestamp":"2026-08-31T07:14:50.234Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"2:## *\"Read the phase before you read the logs\"*\n13:## Attention Budget\n47:## 🧭 Soundings\n53:1. A Pod's `status` reports a `Reason` string of `ContainerCreating`. Does that string belong to the Pod's **phase** taxonomy or the **container state** taxonomy?\n55:2. A Pod has been sitting in `Pending` for ten minutes. Is some component of the cluster periodically retrying it with relaxed constraints, hoping to eventually place it?\n57:3. A Pod spec names the image `myapp` with no tag at all. What `imagePullPolicy` does Kubernetes apply by default, and when does the kubelet consult the registry?\n59:4. A Pod has two containers. Container A requests 100m CPU and 128Mi memory, with limits of 200m and 256Mi. Container B requests 50m CPU and 64Mi memory, with no limits at all. What QoS class does this Pod receive?\n61:5. A node reports `Ready=False`. What is that condition actually telling you, and which component wrote it?\n63:6. Your control plane runs Kubernetes 1.37. What is the oldest kubelet minor version that is supported against it?\n65:7. A Pod runs three containers: `app`, `cache`, and `log-shipper`. How do you ask for the logs of `cache` specifically?\n67:8. Someone creates an Ingress object on a cluster where no Ingress controller has ever been installed. Does the object get created? Does anything route traffic?\n69:<details>\n72:**1.** Container state. `Reason` is a field on the container state, not on the Pod phase. The five Pod phases are `Pending`, `Running`, `Succeeded`, `Failed`, `Unknown`, and none of them is called `ContainerCreating`. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 §5 — Pod phases and container states]*\n74:**2.** No. Nothing is retrying it with looser constraints. `Pending` is a stable report that no feasible node was found. The scheduler will place the Pod the moment a node becomes feasible, but it will not relax the Pod's own requirements to make that happen. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 7 §2 — feasible nodes]*\n76:**3.** With no tag, Kubernetes assumes `:latest`, and the default `imagePullPolicy` for `:latest` is `Always`. The kubelet queries the registry every time it launches a container. [source: k8s-docs-images-2026-08-23] *[cross-bearing: see Ch 2 §6 — imagePullPolicy]*\n78:**4.** `Burstable`. It is not `Guaranteed`, because container B has no limits and container A's limits do not equal its requests. It is not `BestEffort`, because at least one container has requests. [source: k8s-docs-pod-qos-2026-08-24] *[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 §8 — QoS classes]*\n80:**5.** `Ready=False` means the node is not healthy and is not accepting Pods. The kubelet reports node status; the node controller sets `Ready` to `Unknown` if it stops hearing from the node entirely. [source: k8s-docs-node-status-2026-08-24] *[cross-bearing: see Ch 8 §4 — node conditions]*\n82:**6.** 1.34. The kubelet may be up to three minor versions older than the API server, and must never be newer. [source: k8s-version-skew-policy-2026-08-31] *[cross-bearing: see Ch 8 §6 — the version-skew window]*\n84:**7.** `kubectl logs <pod> -c cache`. Without `-c`, the command has to guess, and on a multi-container Pod it will refuse or pick the first container rather than the one you meant. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 §2 — multi-container Pods]*\n86:**8.** The object is created. The API server accepts it and stores it. Nothing routes traffic, because no controller is watching for Ingress objects and acting on them. The object is a record of intent with nobody to honor it. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 10 §3 — the object exists; nothing happens without the component]*\n96:</details>\n100:## Why This Chapter Matters\n128:## What You'll Learn\n143:## ⚪ §1 — Whose Problem Is This, and What to Read First\n198:## 🔵 §2 — Pods That Never Start\n331:## ⚪ §3 — Looking Inside\n451:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings: Pods That Never Start, and the Commands That Tell You Why\n455:**1.** A Pod's containers show `Reason: CreateContainerConfigError`. Which of the following is definitely true?\n462:**2.** You run `kubectl get pods` and see a Pod with `STATUS: ImagePullBackOff` and `RESTARTS: 0`. What does the restart count of zero tell you?\n469:**3.** A Pod has been in `Pending` for twenty minutes. Which single command output most directly tells you why?\n476:**4.** Your `kubectl apply` of a Deployment succeeded, but no Pods exist and the Deployment shows zero available replicas. Where is the explanation most likely to be?\n483:**5.** A Pod spec sets `imagePullPolicy: Never` and names an image that exists in your registry but not on the node. What happens?\n490:**6.** *[retrieval: ch2]* You want a Pod to run one exact, unchangeable build of an image, with no possibility that a moved pointer silently gives you different content. Do you reference the image by tag or by digest, and why?\n499:<details>\n569:</details>\n573:## 🔵 §4 — Pods That Start and Then Do