{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BnXwf1VhpGLAQwy3u6nFwM","type":"tool_result","content":"383:## 🧭 Soundings\n384-\n385-Before reading this chapter, try these eight questions. Your score sets your reading strategy. There is no wrong score, only different approaches.\n386-\n387-Six of these test material from earlier chapters. Two of them, questions 6 and 7, ask you to reason from professional intuition you may have built outside Kubernetes entirely. Answer both kinds honestly; the second kind is doing more work than it looks like.\n388-\n389-1. A resource is cluster-scoped rather than namespaced. Can a permission over it be granted \"inside one namespace\"? Is your answer a matter of policy, or is it forced by something structural?\n390-\n391-2. A Pod's manifest names no ServiceAccount. What identity does the Pod run as, and where did that identity come from?\n392-\n393-3. A Secret's values are base64-encoded. What does base64 do to those values, and — stated as plainly as Chapter 4 stated it — what does it *not* do?\n394-\n395-4. Name the three gates an API request passes through, in order. Which of the three runs last?\n396-\n397-5. Can one NetworkPolicy deny traffic that a different NetworkPolicy allows?\n398-\n399-6. Think of a permission system you have actually used: Unix file modes, a cloud IAM policy, a Windows ACL, a firewall ruleset. In that system, can one rule *subtract* access that another rule grants?\n400-\n401-7. A software release is signed. What does that signature prove, and what does it not prove? Would a signature that covers a *tag* mean the same thing as one that covers a *digest*?\n402-\n403-8. A process is running as root inside a container. Is that the same root as the node's? What does Chapter 2 §1 tell you that bears on the answer?\n404-\n405-<details>\n406-<summary>Answers + reading strategy</summary>\n407-"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"58ff69ea-af14-4b88-8694-2fdf821db391","uuid":"e6b3f94e-bc56-4d87-ba26-725162cf9cf1","timestamp":"2026-08-31T07:17:19.003Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"383:## 🧭 Soundings\n384-\n385-Before reading this chapter, try these eight questions. Your score sets your reading strategy. There is no wrong score, only different approaches.\n386-\n387-Six of these test material from earlier chapters. Two of them, questions 6 and 7, ask you to reason from professional intuition you may have built outside Kubernetes entirely. Answer both kinds honestly; the second kind is doing more work than it looks like.\n388-\n389-1. A resource is cluster-scoped rather than namespaced. Can a permission over it be granted \"inside one namespace\"? Is your answer a matter of policy, or is it forced by something structural?\n390-\n391-2. A Pod's manifest names no ServiceAccount. What identity does the Pod run as, and where did that identity come from?\n392-\n393-3. A Secret's values are base64-encoded. What does base64 do to those values, and — stated as plainly as Chapter 4 stated it — what does it *not* do?\n394-\n395-4. Name the three gates an API request passes through, in order. Which of the three runs last?\n396-\n397-5. Can one NetworkPolicy deny traffic that a different NetworkPolicy allows?\n398-\n399-6. Think of a permission system you have actually used: Unix file modes, a cloud IAM policy, a Windows ACL, a firewall ruleset. In that system, can one rule *subtract* access that another rule grants?\n400-\n401-7. A software release is signed. What does that signature prove, and what does it not prove? Would a signature that covers a *tag* mean the same thing as one that covers a *digest*?\n402-\n403-8. A process is running as root inside a container. Is that the same root as the node's? What does Chapter 2 §1 tell you that bears on the answer?\n404-\n405-<details>\n406-<summary>Answers + reading strategy</summary>\n407-","numLines":25,"totalLines":25}}
